  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "If $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{14}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2.\n$$",
